    "turn_text": "by ads outside the UK. You're listening to a podcast from the BBC World Service. This is HardTalk with me, Stephen Sacker. Thanks for downloading this edition of the program. I do hope you enjoy it. Welcome to HardTalk on the BBC World Service with me, Stephen Sacker. My guest today is a key player in America's Republican Party, not least because his power base is the vital electoral swing state of Florida. Rick Scott was Florida's governor. Now he's one of the state's two US senators, thanks to the narrowest of victories in last November's midterm elections. Back in 2016, Rick Scott was a key backer of Donald Trump's presidential campaign, but in recent times he's put some noticeable distance between himself and the White House, notably on the hugely controversial issue of immigration. President's zero tolerance stance on illegal immigration has led to family separations at the Mexican border and the detention of young children in shocking conditions. Democrats call it an affront to American values. Senator Scott, doubtless mindful of Florida's millions of Latino voters, has voiced his own concerns. The Senator is currently in Europe discussing America's place in the world with European allies, at the time when President Trump's approach to international diplomacy, from China trade to Iran to North Korea, has again raised questions from Washington's traditional partners. So just how effective and sustainable is the Trump presidency? Well, Senator Rick Scott joins me now. Welcome to Hard Talk. It's nice to be here. You've been in Europe for a few days. You've been meeting politicians. You've been meeting diplomats. How hard is it being a senior US politician defending the Trump administration overseas right now?",
